      {
        "slide": 5,
        "role": "evidence",
        "subject": "Two stacks of kraft-wrapped paper reams on an oak table, each turned about 30 degrees. The left stack is fiscal 2027: three reams under a block of loose cream sheets. The right stack is fiscal 2032: eight reams with a torn wrapper on the top ream. A ruler with tick marks stands upright beside the taller stack, in front of a lit muntin window and fielded violet wainscot.",
        "visual_consequence": "Without the headline, one pile of the same paper stands a bit over twice as tall as the other at the same depth. The reams can be counted, three against eight, so the loss reads as more than doubling across the years shown, in the document's own material rather than as bars. Each ream's end face is lit warm kraft with shaded end-fold flaps and a vertical wrapper seam, each long face is in rose shade with a dark band, a cream sheet-edge line runs between reams, and the band meets the corner. The torn wrapper edge and the loose sheet block both read as paper. Contact shadows seat both bases, and the cast shadows fall back and to the right.",
        "weakness": "Three secondary defects; the reams themselves read as finished. (1) At 100 percent the end-fold flaps are slightly translucent, so the rose band shows faintly through the flap triangles on every end face. (2) The steel rule is a flat lilac-pink strip with no visible thickness or edge face, square to the camera while the stacks are turned 30 degrees, with horizontal streaking. (3) The table's grain is a fan of thin, hair-like streaks rather than oak figure, and the far edge meets the wainscot as a hard horizontal cut. The type wash behind the kicker and counter lays a muddy brown band across the peach glass.",
        "repair": "none required for acceptance. If polished later, make the fold flaps opaque so the band wraps over them, and give the rule a steel albedo, an edge thickness and the stacks' 30 degree turn.",
